    effective_date:'2026-09-30', deadline:'2026-09-30',
    what_changed:'The EU AI Office published the final draft of the GPAI Code of Practice under Articles 53–56 of the EU AI Act. The Code consolidates transparency, copyright compliance, risk assessment, and safety measures into a single voluntary compliance instrument. Signatories benefit from a presumption of conformity with the corresponding EU AI Act obligations, eliminating the need to demonstrate equivalent compliance separately.',
    previous_position:'Three iterative working drafts (December 2024, March 2025, July 2025) had been circulated for multi-stakeholder consultation. No final text was in force. Providers operated under self-assessment against the raw statutory text, with no standardised documentation template.',
    new_position:'Final draft text establishes: (1) copyright provenance obligations requiring GPAI providers to implement policies for training data sourced from EU rights-holders, including opt-out mechanisms; (2) a standard technical documentation template aligned with Article 53 requirements; (3) systematic risk assessment obligations for systemic risk threshold models (≥10²³ FLOPs); (4) an incident notification procedure with a 72-hour initial report and 30-day root-cause follow-up to the AI Office. Adoption is voluntary but regulatory reliance expected from Q4 2026.',
    legal_significance:'Signatories are presumed conformant with Articles 53–55 obligations. Non-signatories must demonstrate equivalent compliance individually on regulatory request. No direct penalty for non-adoption, but regulatory risk profile is elevated for non-signatories during market surveillance and enforcement proceedings.',
    business_significance:'GPAI providers distributing models in the EU face a binary compliance path: sign the Code and gain presumption of conformity, or maintain bespoke documentation sufficient to satisfy national authorities on audit. Providers with large copyright-exposed training sets must decide whether to disclose training data sources or undertake remediation before adoption. The copyright provenance obligation is the most commercially sensitive new element.',
    technical_significance:'Documentation pipeline must be able to produce: training data provenance records, model card in AI Office template format, capability evaluation results for the six designated systemic risk categories (biosecurity, cybersecurity, CBRN, democratic processes, financial stability, critical infrastructure), and incident detection telemetry. Quarterly capability re-evaluation required for designated systemic risk models.',
