    effective_date:'2026-08-28', deadline:'',
    what_changed:'NIST published a sector-specific Healthcare Profile for the AI Risk Management Framework. The Profile maps 48 AI RMF sub-categories to healthcare-specific practices, identifies 12 healthcare-unique risks including diagnostic AI bias across patient demographics, AI-driven formulary manipulation, and model drift in chronic-disease management, and recommends 31 implementation activities absent from the general framework.',
    previous_position:'Only the general AI RMF (AI 100-1, 2023) applied. Healthcare organizations adapted it informally without standardised guidance. FDA AI/ML guidance existed separately with limited RMF cross-reference, creating a compliance interpretation gap.',
    new_position:'Dedicated Healthcare Profile standardises AI governance expectations for health systems, device manufacturers, and insurers. Profile introduces: demographic equity measurement requirements for diagnostic AI disaggregated by age bracket, self-reported sex, and self-reported race/ethnicity; mandatory adversarial testing for AI triaging or treatment-recommendation systems; post-market monitoring cadences aligned with FDA 510(k) cycles; and supply-chain AI bill of materials requirements for clinical-grade deployments. Quarterly performance re-evaluation with automated alert thresholds is the recommended monitoring cadence.',
    legal_significance:'Voluntary framework. However, HHS Office for Civil Rights references the AI RMF in non-discrimination guidance; FDA may incorporate Profile recommendations into AI/ML SaMD guidance updates. State-level algorithmic accountability legislation in California, Illinois, and Colorado already mandates RMF-aligned documentation, making the Profile indirectly enforceable in those jurisdictions.',
    business_significance:'Health systems seeking HITRUST or SOC 2 + AI attestation will find auditors referencing the Profile from Q1 2027. Medical device manufacturers must assess whether their AI/ML documentation practices align before the next FDA review cycle. Clinical AI vendors must prepare to demonstrate demographic equity testing on request from health system procurement teams, which are increasingly embedding algorithmic bias requirements in vendor contracts.',
    technical_significance:'Introduces standardised bias evaluation methodology with minimum disaggregation by three demographic dimensions. Model drift monitoring standard: minimum quarterly performance re-evaluation with automated alert thresholds defined per condition type. Supply-chain SBOM for AI requires component-level disclosure of pre-trained model provenance, fine-tuning datasets, and evaluation results — a substantially higher documentation standard than current practice.',
