    new_position:'Consultation proposes mandatory status for: (1) accountability and governance, (2) human oversight and control, (3) testing and evaluation, (4) transparency, (5) bias and fairness, (6) privacy, (7) security, (8) contestability and redress, (9) responsible deployment, and (10) ongoing monitoring. Enforcement through a proposed AI Regulatory Council with infringement notice and civil penalty powers up to A$50M. Sector-specific safe harbours proposed for AI systems certified under designated international frameworks including ISO 42001 and EU AI Act CE marking.',
    legal_significance:'If enacted as proposed: civil penalties up to A$50M for large organisations and A$10M for small organisations. Extraterritorial reach proposed for AI systems affecting Australian residents operated by offshore entities. The ISO 42001 and EU AI Act safe harbour provisions create immediate strategic value for multinationals already pursuing those certifications.',
    business_significance:'Approximately 2,400 organisations exceed the A$50M revenue threshold in designated sectors. Compliance roadmap requires a gap assessment against all ten guardrails, a governance structure with a named AI accountability officer, and testing documentation infrastructure. The consultation response window closes 18 October 2026; industry submissions will materially shape the final scope.',
    technical_significance:'Guardrail 3 (testing) requires pre-deployment adversarial testing, structured red-team exercises, and post-deployment monitoring plans. Guardrail 6 (privacy) requires an AI-specific impact assessment equivalent to a Privacy Impact Assessment. Guardrail 10 (ongoing monitoring) requires automated drift detection and quarterly reporting to designated internal oversight function.',
