    what_changed:'Innovation, Science and Economic Development Canada published proposed regulations under the Artificial Intelligence and Data Act (AIDA) in the Canada Gazette. The regulations define "high-impact AI system" across seven domains (healthcare, employment, justice, financial services, fundamental freedoms, critical infrastructure, and education); set out risk assessment, impact assessment, and monitoring obligations; and establish the AI and Data Commissioner\'s enforcement framework including audits, corrective orders, and administrative monetary penalties.',
    previous_position:'AIDA (Bill C-27 Part III) received Royal Assent in June 2024. However, all industry obligations under AIDA were suspended pending publication of regulations. No duties applied to any organisation in the interim period. The absence of regulations created compliance uncertainty that prevented meaningful voluntary preparation.',
    new_position:'Proposed regulations define the full operative framework. High-impact AI: covers seven sectors, with GPAI models ≥10²³ FLOPs or achieving a capability threshold on three or more of eight listed benchmarks as a separate category. Risk assessments: mandatory annual, retained for 10 years. Impact assessments: biannual for deployed systems, retained for the lifetime of the system. Incident reporting: 10-day initial report to Commissioner for any AI-caused serious harm. Enforcement: audits on Commissioner initiative, corrective orders, administrative monetary penalties up to C$25M or 3% of global turnover per contravention.',
    legal_significance:'Royal Assent obtained. Proposed regulations are the final trigger. Once finalised (estimated Q1 2027), AIDA obligations apply immediately without a transition period. Criminal penalties for intentional non-compliance: up to C$25M or 3% of global revenue. The 10-year documentation retention obligation is among the longest in any comparable regime.',
    business_significance:'Multinational operators with Canadian revenue or Canadian users must conduct an AIDA scoping assessment before Q1 2027. Entities already compliant with EU AI Act high-risk obligations will have substantial overlap but must verify Canadian-specific differences including definition scope, incident reporting timelines (10 days vs. 72 hours), and documentation retention requirements. The comment period provides an unusually long window — 60 days — for substantive engagement.',
    technical_significance:'Regulations introduce a "meaningful human control" standard for high-impact decisions: automated outcomes must be challengeable by a natural person with access to a human reviewer within 30 days of the decision. Technical systems must support audit trails for each high-impact decision output, retained for a minimum of 10 years. AI SBOM: component-level disclosure of pre-trained model provenance, fine-tuning datasets, and evaluation results required for GPAI threshold models.',
