    what_changed:'The Japanese House of Councillors Special Committee on the Digital Society tabled a substantially revised AI Promotion Act draft that converts Japan\'s prior voluntary framework into a statutory one. The revised draft introduces: (1) a binding safety assessment requirement for designated high-risk AI systems before commercial deployment, conducted by a designated third-party body; (2) an AI Incident Registry operated by MIC with mandatory operator reporting within 30 days of any AI-caused significant harm; (3) a GPAI developer duty of care standard for frontier models available in Japan, requiring quarterly capability reports; and (4) an AI provenance label requirement for AI-generated media content in regulated contexts.',
    previous_position:'Japan operated under the AI Guidelines for Business (April 2024) and the Hiroshima AI Process voluntary framework. No mandatory safety assessment or incident registry existed. The AI Promotion Act (enacted May 2024) covered only promotion and investment incentives, not safety obligations. The absence of binding rules was an internationally noted gap given Japan\'s active AI deployment in manufacturing and critical infrastructure.',
    new_position:'Revised draft, if enacted: designates six high-risk AI categories requiring pre-deployment assessment by a designated third-party body (critical infrastructure AI, biometric AI, employment AI, healthcare AI, justice AI, education AI). MIC establishes and maintains a national AI Incident Registry. GPAI duty of care: developers must publish quarterly capability reports and cooperate with government capability evaluations on request. Media provenance: AI-generated label required in news, advertising, and public-information AI outputs under a Japanese-standard watermarking regime. Estimated enactment: Q2 2027.',
    legal_significance:'If enacted: creates the first comprehensive mandatory AI safety framework in Japan. Enforcement authority vested in MIC with civil penalty and corrective order powers. Extraterritorial application proposed for operators providing AI services in Japan from abroad. Third-party assessment requirement for high-risk categories creates a new conformity market.',
    business_significance:'Global AI developers with Japanese revenue or user base must monitor bill progress closely. Pre-deployment assessment requirement could create a 3–6 month delay for new high-risk AI launches in Japan pending accreditation of third-party assessment bodies. GPAI quarterly capability reports represent a significant documentation burden for frontier model providers. Media provenance requirement affects AI content platforms serving Japanese users.',
    technical_significance:'Pre-deployment assessment requires structured red-team evaluation, adversarial robustness testing, and output audit trails retained for five years. AI provenance label: C2PA or Japanese equivalent watermarking standard to be designated by MIC. Incident registry API: machine-readable incident reports in STIX-compatible format proposed in the technical annex.',
