    effective_date:'2026-09-15', deadline:'2026-08-02',
    what_changed:'The European Commission published the first wave of formally designated notified bodies for third-party conformity assessment of high-risk AI systems under Article 33 of the EU AI Act. Fourteen bodies across seven Member States were granted designation, covering all Annex III high-risk categories including biometric identification, critical infrastructure management, education, employment, essential private services, law enforcement, migration management, and administration of justice.',
    previous_position:'No notified bodies had been formally designated for EU AI Act conformity assessment as of August 2026. The first deadline for mandatory third-party conformity assessment of Annex III high-risk AI systems — 2 August 2026 — had passed without functioning notified body infrastructure. The Commission granted a temporary regulatory accommodation acknowledging the structural gap, but providers could not demonstrate full compliance during the gap period.',
    new_position:'Fourteen notified bodies designated across Germany (five, including TÜV SÜD AI Lab and TÜV Rheinland AI Conformity), France (three), the Netherlands (two), Sweden (two), Italy (one), and Denmark (one). All Annex III high-risk categories covered collectively. The Commission is processing a further twelve applications (wave 2 expected Q4 2026). Providers who placed high-risk AI on the market before 2 August 2026 must complete third-party conformity assessment by 2 August 2027 under the transitional provisions now activated.',
    legal_significance:'Removes the principal practical obstacle to third-party conformity assessment. Providers who deferred compliance pending notified body availability no longer have a legitimate deferral argument. The 2027 transitional deadline is now enforceable against a functioning notified body infrastructure, creating defined exposure for non-compliant high-risk AI already on the EU market.',
    business_significance:'High-risk AI providers should initiate notified body engagement immediately. Booking windows are estimated at 3–6 months given constrained supply of fourteen designated bodies against demand from providers across all Annex III sectors. Documentation packages must be complete before booking to avoid wasted lead time. Published cost benchmark: €40,000–€180,000 per conformity assessment engagement depending on system complexity, category, and body.',
    technical_significance:'Technical file (Annex IV) required for assessment: system architecture documentation, training and test data governance records, performance monitoring framework, human oversight measures and implementation evidence, risk management documentation aligned with ISO 31000, sample testing records, and CE marking declaration of conformity. Quality management system must be ISO 9001:2015 compatible and include AI-specific procedures as specified in the EU AI Act.',
