    new_position:"Standardised 68-dimension evaluation framework effective from January 2027. Six evaluation categories with weight allocations: safety behaviors (25%), harmful content (22%), factual accuracy (20%), adversarial robustness (15%), bias and fairness (10%), privacy and data security (8%). CNEVAL-LLM benchmark suite: 14,000 test prompts across 68 dimensions, available in simplified and traditional Chinese. Quarterly public disclosure: providers must publish CNEVAL-LLM results on CAC's public registry. Third-party audit mandatory for systems used in news, finance, medicine, law, or government through a CAC-designated testing institution.",
    legal_significance:'Voluntary standard at publication but with anticipated mandatory conversion via CAC rulemaking in 2027. Third-party evaluation obligation for designated sector applications is already mandatory under existing Generative AI Measures as clarified by reference to the new standard. Non-compliance with quarterly disclosure obligations constitutes a violation of the Generative AI Measures.',
    business_significance:'Chinese LLM providers must implement CNEVAL-LLM evaluation infrastructure before Q1 2027. Global LLMs targeting the Chinese market must assess CNEVAL-LLM performance before filing CAC registration applications. The standard creates a new ongoing compliance cost layer — quarterly evaluation and public reporting — but also provides a clear, measurable compliance pathway that replaces the previous ambiguous self-assessment regime.',
    technical_significance:'CNEVAL-LLM benchmark: 14,000 test prompts across 68 dimensions. Harmful content detection requires both automated classifier scoring and human review sampling of model outputs on a defined statistical sample. Privacy evaluation includes membership inference attack resistance testing and PII leakage detection. Adversarial robustness: 12 prompt injection and jailbreak test categories. Results must be retained for three years and submitted to CAC on request.',
