    effective_date:'2026-09-17', deadline:'',
    what_changed:'The EU AI Office published its first formal designations of GPAI models as presenting systemic risk under Article 51 of the EU AI Act. Seven models from five providers were designated: GPT-4o and o3 (OpenAI), Gemini Ultra 2.0 (Google DeepMind), Claude Opus 4 (Anthropic), Llama 3.2 405B hosted commercial version (Meta), Mistral Large 2 (Mistral AI), and Grok-2 (xAI). Providers of designated models must now comply with the full obligations under Articles 51–55 including adversarial testing, incident reporting, technical documentation, and GPAI Code of Practice adherence within 90 days.',
    previous_position:'The 10²³ FLOPs training computation threshold applied automatically per Article 51(1), but no formal designation decision had been published identifying specific models by name. Providers operated under a self-assessment regime with legal uncertainty about which models triggered systemic risk obligations. The absence of formal designation created enforcement ambiguity.',
    new_position:'Seven models formally designated. Providers of designated models have 90 days to demonstrate compliance with Articles 51–55 or submit a remediation plan to the AI Office. Ongoing obligations include: annual adversarial testing by a designated red-team body from the AI Office approved list, quarterly incident reports to the AI Office, technical documentation on the systemic risk assessment and its annual update, and GPAI Code of Practice adherence or demonstration of equivalent compliance. A further twelve models are under assessment; the next designation wave is expected Q1 2027.',
    legal_significance:'Formal designation creates legally binding obligations with defined timelines. Penalties for non-compliance by systemically risky GPAI providers: up to €35M or 7% of global annual turnover, the highest penalty tier in the EU AI Act. Designation places each named provider on formal regulatory notice — any subsequent non-compliance occurs against an explicit public regulatory record that constitutes prior notice.',
    business_significance:'OpenAI, Google DeepMind, Anthropic, Meta, Mistral AI, and xAI must now treat their designated models as subject to the full systemic risk regime. Compliance workstreams required: adversarial testing engagement with an AI Office approved red-team body (published cost estimate €2–5M per evaluation cycle for large frontier models), incident detection infrastructure, quarterly reporting processes, and annual technical documentation review with internal legal and technical sign-off.',
    technical_significance:'Adversarial testing scope: six risk categories — biosecurity, cybersecurity, CBRN weapons, democratic process integrity, financial system stability, and critical infrastructure. Testing methodology must include both automated adversarial attacks and structured red-team exercises with human expert involvement. Incident definition: any event where a designated model was causally involved in harm above a defined severity threshold, whether deliberately exploited or encountered in normal deployment. Incident reports must include a root-cause analysis submitted within 30 days of initial notification.',
