    actors_affected:['Frontier model developer','AI deployer (G7 jurisdiction)','National AI safety authority'],
    risk_category:'Frontier AI / systemic risk',
    effective_date:'2026-09-18', deadline:'',
    what_changed:'At the September 2026 Osaka Digital Ministers meeting, G7 members adopted a joint declaration establishing: (1) a shared baseline evaluation methodology for frontier AI safety testing aligned across G7 national frameworks; (2) a mutual recognition framework allowing safety testing results validated by one G7 national AI safety authority to satisfy equivalent requirements in other G7 jurisdictions; (3) a cross-border AI incident notification mechanism with a 48-hour initial alert obligation between national authorities for AI-related incidents with cross-border consequences; and (4) a G7 AI Safety Testing Interoperability Registry listing approved evaluation bodies and their recognised scope across G7 jurisdictions.',
    previous_position:'Each G7 jurisdiction operated independent frontier AI evaluation requirements — AISI (UK), NIST AI Safety Institute (US), Japan\'s AISI, EU AI Office, and others. No mutual recognition existed, requiring frontier model developers to conduct substantially duplicative evaluations for each jurisdiction. No G7-level incident coordination mechanism existed.',
    new_position:'Mutual recognition of safety evaluation results for frontier models tested by a G7-listed approved body, effective immediately for declaration signatories. Shared evaluation methodology covers: capability evaluations across biosecurity, cybersecurity, autonomous replication, and critical infrastructure uplift domains; red-team exercise standards; and documentation requirements. Incident notification: 48-hour authority-to-authority alert for designated cross-border AI incidents; 30-day detailed incident report. Interoperability Registry operational from Q4 2026.',
    legal_significance:'Ministerial declarations are not treaties and create no binding international legal obligations. However, each G7 jurisdiction is expected to incorporate the shared methodology and mutual recognition principles into domestic implementation. UK AISI, NIST, and the EU AI Office have each confirmed compatibility with their existing frameworks, reducing duplication burden for designated providers.',
    business_significance:'Frontier model developers currently face duplicative evaluation costs across G7 jurisdictions — estimated at €2–8M per evaluation cycle per jurisdiction. The mutual recognition framework will allow a single G7-accredited evaluation to satisfy multiple jurisdictions\' requirements in parallel once the Interoperability Registry is operational in Q4 2026. Developers with evaluations already underway should check whether their evaluation body qualifies for G7 registry listing.',
    technical_significance:'Shared methodology introduces three standardised test battery components: a CBRN capability evaluation protocol, an autonomous replication test protocol in sandboxed cloud environments, and a critical infrastructure uplift evaluation using agreed scenario sets. Developers must ensure evaluation logs are structured to satisfy the documentation requirements of all G7 jurisdictions in a single evaluation run — a unified logging format specification is being finalised by the Interoperability Registry working group.',
