    effective_date:'Pending presidential signature — expected promulgation within 15 days', deadline:'',
    what_changed:'The Brazilian Senate approved the Lei Geral de Inteligência Artificial (LGAI) on 16 September 2026. The law establishes a risk-based AI governance framework covering the development, deployment, and use of AI systems in Brazil. Key provisions: a three-tier risk classification (unacceptable, high, limited risk); mandatory conformity assessment for high-risk AI before deployment; an AI Transparency Register operated by the Federal Data Protection Authority (ANPD); prohibited AI practices including manipulation through subliminal techniques, systematic discrimination, and social scoring by public authorities; civil penalties up to 2% of Brazilian revenue (capped at BRL 50M); and a national AI incident reporting obligation.',
    previous_position:'Brazil had no AI-specific legislation. AI use was governed under the General Data Protection Law (LGPD, 2018) and general consumer protection and constitutional principles. Several AI governance bills had been debated since 2021 without enactment. The Senate had approved an earlier version in December 2023 which was returned to the Chamber; the Senate passed the reconciled final text on 16 September 2026.',
    new_position:'High-risk AI categories include: AI in criminal justice decisions, healthcare triage or diagnosis, access to financial services, education assessment, employment decisions, and critical infrastructure management. Mandatory pre-deployment conformity assessment for high-risk AI through ANPD-accredited bodies. AI Transparency Register: all high-risk AI systems must register before deployment in Brazil. Prohibited practices carry criminal penalties up to 3 years imprisonment for company officers in addition to civil fines. ANPD enforcement body created with investigative, corrective order, and penalty powers. Transition periods: 24 months for existing systems; 12 months for new systems.',
    legal_significance:'Creates comprehensive binding AI obligations for the first time in Brazil. Extraterritorial reach: applies to any AI system that produces effects in Brazil regardless of provider location. Interaction with LGPD: the LGAI supplements the LGPD — AI systems affecting personal data rights are subject to both regimes simultaneously. Pending Presidential signature expected within 15 days of the September 16 Senate vote.',
    business_significance:'Brazil is the largest economy in Latin America. The mandatory conformity assessment requirement creates immediate market access obligations for international AI providers. ANPD will operate the registration system; accredited conformity assessment bodies will be designated within 12 months. Companies with high-risk AI products deployed or planned in Brazil must initiate compliance scoping immediately — the 12-month period for new systems begins on the promulgation date.',
    technical_significance:'Conformity assessment technical file requires: system architecture documentation, training data governance, performance metrics disaggregated by protected demographic attributes, human oversight implementation evidence, risk management documentation, and adversarial testing results. The AI Transparency Register API specification will be published within 6 months of promulgation. Post-market monitoring: annual re-assessment required for deployed high-risk AI.',
