    industries:[], ai_system_type:'High-risk AI systems, AI systems affecting public interest',
    actors_affected:['AI developer (India-based or India-market)','AI deployer','Digital intermediary','Critical infrastructure operator'],
    risk_category:'National AI governance',
    effective_date:'Pending — consultation closes 2026-11-15', deadline:'2026-11-15',
    what_changed:'India\'s Ministry of Electronics and Information Technology (MeitY) published a draft binding AI governance framework for public consultation. The draft introduces a five-tier AI risk classification, mandatory AI impact assessments for Tier 3–5 systems, an AI regulatory sandbox operated by a proposed Digital India AI Authority (DIAIA), and a National AI Incident Registry with 30-day mandatory reporting. The draft marks a shift from India\'s prior voluntary approach towards a binding regulatory regime.',
    previous_position:'India operated under voluntary NITI Aayog AI governance principles and the 2021 National AI Strategy. No binding AI-specific legislation existed. MeitY had stated publicly as recently as February 2026 that India intended to remain light-touch on AI regulation.',
    new_position:'Draft proposes five-tier risk classification: Tier 1 (minimal) — no obligations; Tier 2 (limited) — transparency disclosure; Tier 3 (moderate) — mandatory AI Impact Assessment; Tier 4 (significant) — AI Impact Assessment plus pre-deployment DIAIA review; Tier 5 (high) — full third-party conformity assessment. High-risk sectors: critical infrastructure, healthcare, financial services, law enforcement, employment, education, and government decision-making. Prohibited practices: social scoring by public authorities, biometric mass surveillance in public spaces, subliminal manipulation. AI regulatory sandbox open from Q1 2027. National AI Incident Registry: 30-day reporting for AI-caused significant harm. Consultation closes 15 November 2026.',
    legal_significance:'Draft only — not yet binding. However, the shift from voluntary to mandatory signals imminent legislative action. India\'s market scale (1.4 billion population, third-largest digital economy) means that a binding framework, if enacted with the proposed scope, would create compliance obligations comparable in scale to the EU AI Act for global AI providers. DIAIA creation requires legislative authority; the draft anticipates a Digital India AI Governance Act within 12–18 months.',
    business_significance:'Global AI providers operating in India or targeting Indian users should treat this consultation as a material early warning. The 30-day incident reporting obligation would be the fastest mandatory timeline globally if enacted. Developers already EU AI Act compliant will find substantial overlap with the five-tier classification but must assess Indian-specific differences including data localisation requirements for Tier 4–5 systems.',
    technical_significance:'AI Impact Assessment (Tier 3–5) must include bias and fairness evaluation disaggregated across gender, caste, religion, and economic status — demographic dimensions specific to India\'s constitutional equal protection framework. Data localisation: Tier 4–5 systems processing Indian personal data must store training data records in India. Sandbox technical environment: DIAIA will provide isolated testing environments for novel AI systems seeking pre-market regulatory clearance.',
