    published_date:'2026-09-15',
    last_updated:'2026-09-15',
    reviewer:'Jean-Daniel Alexis',
    overall_score:8.2,
    verdict:'The most capable AI-native research tool for professionals who need cited, real-time web intelligence. Perplexity\'s source citation is genuinely reliable — 97% of URLs accessible at test date, zero invented citations on mainstream topics — and the Focus: Academic mode substantially improves source quality for research-grade queries. The accuracy ceiling is limited by the quality of web search results and multi-source synthesis, not the underlying model. For a $1,500/year platform subscriber who needs to track regulatory developments, Perplexity Pro is the fastest research tool in the category. The 18% accuracy gap on niche queries means findings should be verified against primary sources before reliance.',
    scores:[
      {dimension:'RKA-01', name:'Source quality & citation', score:8.5, note:'Industry-leading source citation with inline numbered references on every claim. 97% of cited URLs accessible at test date. The proportion of primary sources and peer-reviewed publications is above the category average, particularly in Academic focus mode. Zero invented citations detected on mainstream topics. Niche technical queries occasionally surface secondary commentary as the top citation when primary sources would be more authoritative.'},
      {dimension:'RKA-02', name:'Factual accuracy',          score:8.0, note:'Strong on current events and recent regulatory developments given live web access — measurably better than training-data-only models on queries about events in the past 12 months. Some drift on historical nuance where web search results are dominated by secondary commentary. Corrects false-premise questions in 80% of test cases. The 18% failure rate on niche domain queries is the most significant buyer risk to communicate.'},
      {dimension:'RKA-03', name:'Query comprehension',       score:8.5, note:'Handles comparative and multi-part queries accurately, with structured output that addresses each part. Follow-up conversation correctly builds on prior context within a thread. The model rarely asks for clarification on ambiguous queries — it tends to proceed with a reasonable interpretation. This works well for expert users who frame queries precisely; it can produce off-target results for ambiguous queries from non-expert users.'},
      {dimension:'RKA-04', name:'Reasoning depth',           score:7.5, note:'Synthesis is accurate but tends toward structured summarisation rather than original analytical reasoning. On comparative policy questions (e.g., EU vs. UK AI governance frameworks), the output correctly identifies differences but does not assess implications without explicit prompting. Model selection (Claude 3.5 Sonnet or GPT-4o in Pro mode) materially improves reasoning depth on complex analytical queries.'},
      {dimension:'RKA-05', name:'Transparency & limitations', score:8.5, note:'Search sources always displayed inline and at the end of responses. AI-generated nature of the synthesis is always clear. Knowledge cutoff appropriately noted when queries touch training data vs. web search. Limitations communicated for out-of-scope queries. The highest transparency score in the knowledge-agent category because source visibility is structural, not optional.'}
    ],
